NAME
    netfs - mount a directory shared by another TOS machine

SYNOPSIS
    netfs status
    netfs exports
    netfs mount <host> <share> <mountpoint>     (admin)
    netfs umount <mountpoint>                   (admin)

DESCRIPTION
    netfs attaches a directory that another TOS machine has chosen to
    export, so it appears under your own filesystem. It has two halves
    and they are independent: what THIS machine serves to others, and
    what this machine has MOUNTED from others. `netfs status` shows both.

    A share lives on someone else's computer. When that machine is off,
    the mount is still there but empty, and its files disappear from a
    listing without having been deleted. This is the same bargain a
    Windows folder share makes, and it is the main thing to understand
    before relying on one.

    Mount points must be under /mnt/, so a remote share can never shadow
    a system path.

SERVING
    This machine serves nothing until BOTH of these are true:

      1. /etc/netfs-exports.cfg lists at least one export, and
      2. the netfsd service is running (service start netfsd).

    Stopping netfsd stops serving immediately. There is no state where
    exports are configured and quietly served by a stopped service.

    The export file is serialized data, never executed:

      return {
        { name = "pub",   path = "/srv/pub",   mode = "ro",
          allow = { "a1b2c3d4" } },
        { name = "drop",  path = "/srv/drop",  mode = "rw",
          allow = { "*paired*" } },
      }

    name   short label peers use to ask for the share; [A-Za-z0-9_-].
    path   the directory to export. Exporting / is refused.
    mode   "ro" (default) or "rw". Enforced by the serving machine.
    allow  peer addresses, or "*paired*" for any peer you already trust
           at TRUSTED level. An EMPTY allow list serves nobody -- an
           export with no rule is treated as a mistake, not as public.

    Run `netfs exports` after editing the file: it re-reads it and
    reports any error. A malformed file serves nothing rather than
    falling back to a previous, possibly wider, set.

SECURITY
    Requests are answered only for peers at TRUSTED level, and only
    after a challenge-response check that the peer still holds the
    shared secret -- a trusted modem that has been moved to another
    machine does not pass. Denials on the wire are deliberately
    uninformative so a caller cannot probe for paths or map which peers
    you share secrets with.

    Every path is confined to its export root: ".." is resolved before
    the check, so it cannot climb out, and an export of /srv/pub does
    not also grant /srv/public-extra.

    LIMITATION: confinement is per-export, not per-user. Any allowed
    peer gets everything under the export root at the export's mode.
    TOS user ACLs apply to local users; a remote peer is a modem
    address, not a user. Export narrow directories.

EXAMPLES
    Share a read-only directory with one machine:

      edit /etc/netfs-exports.cfg
      service start netfsd
      netfs status

    Mount it from the other side:

      netfs mount workshop pub /mnt/workshop
      ls /mnt/workshop
      netfs umount /mnt/workshop

    <host> may be a peer hostname or an address prefix, the same forms
    `share` and `ping` accept.

NOTES
    Writes are buffered and are not durable until the file is closed.
    A mount whose host has gone away reports "unreachable" in
    `netfs status` and zero free space.

    A netfs share can also be used as a JBOD pool member, which is how
    several machines' storage can be pooled into one mount. See
    `man jbod`.

ERRORS
    netfs module unavailable          the kernel module is not present
    Peer is not TRUSTED               raise trust first; see `net trust`
    Mount point must be under /mnt/   pick a path under /mnt/
    Cannot reach share '<name>'       wrong export name, netfsd stopped
                                      on the host, or you are not in its
                                      allow list
    Export file rejected: <reason>    /etc/netfs-exports.cfg is invalid;
                                      nothing is served until it parses

SEE ALSO
    jbod, mount, umount, df, net, share, service
